{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The elbow is a synovial hinge joint where the humerus meets the ulna and radius. The humeroulnar and humeroradial articulations allow flexion and extension, about 150 degrees. The proximal radioulnar joint, inside the same capsule, allows pronation and supination. Stability comes from the deep trochlear fit, the collateral ligaments and the annular ligament.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN13.3" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## What kind of joint is the elbow?

The elbow is a **compound synovial joint**. Several articulations share one capsule and one joint cavity, and all the articular surfaces are covered with hyaline cartilage. Three joints together are called the **cubital articulations**:

- The **humeroulnar joint**, between the trochlea of the humerus and the trochlear notch of the ulna
- The **humeroradial joint**, between the capitulum of the humerus and the head of the radius
- The **proximal (superior) radioulnar joint**, between the head of the radius, the radial notch of the ulna and the annular ligament

The humeroulnar and humeroradial parts together act as a **hinge** and produce flexion and extension. The proximal radioulnar joint is a **pivot joint** and, together with the distal radioulnar joint, produces pronation and supination. The elbow joint is continuous with the proximal radioulnar joint.

## Bones and articular surfaces

The lower end of the humerus carries the parts that matter for the elbow:

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/elbow-joint/elbow-bones.webp" alt="Bones of the elbow joint with articular surfaces labelled." caption="The humerus articulates with the ulna and radius at three connected joints." credit="Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons" license="Public domain" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LateralLeftHumerusRadiusUlnaArticulated.jpg" width="960" height="854" /%}

- **Trochlea:** the pulley-shaped surface that fits the trochlear notch of the ulna. Its medial edge projects down about 6 mm more than the lateral edge. This difference forms the **carrying angle**.
- **Capitulum:** the rounded, half-spherical surface (Latin for "little head") on the lateral side, which articulates with the head of the radius.
- **Medial epicondyle:** a prominent, subcutaneous projection that is easy to feel. The ulnar nerve runs in a shallow groove on its back. The superficial forearm flexors arise from its front.
- **Lateral epicondyle:** smaller than the medial one. The superficial forearm extensors arise from it. The lateral epicondyle and capitulum together make up the lateral condyle.

Three fossae above the articular surfaces receive bony processes at the end of a movement:

- The **coronoid fossa** takes the coronoid process of the ulna in flexion.
- The **radial fossa** takes the head of the radius in flexion.
- The **olecranon fossa** takes the olecranon of the ulna in extension.

The two epicondylar muscle groups are **extracapsular**, so they lie outside the joint capsule.

## Capsule and synovial membrane

The capsule encloses the capitulum, the trochlea and the three fossae. It is attached below to the margin of the trochlear notch and to the annular ligament, but it is not attached to the radius itself.

- **Front and back:** the anterior and posterior walls of the capsule are thin and weak. The anterior and posterior ligaments are only thickenings of the capsule.
- **Sides:** the capsule blends into the ulnar collateral ligament medially and the radial collateral ligament laterally.
- **Synovial membrane:** lines the capsule and the fossae, covers the fat pads in the coronoid, radial and olecranon fossae, and is continuous with the synovial membrane of the proximal radioulnar joint. Brachialis pushes the fat pads in the coronoid and radial fossae into their fossae when the elbow extends.
- **Olecranon bursa:** lies between the capsule and the triceps insertion. Pressure, friction or infection can inflame it and cause marked swelling and limited movement.

Because the capsule is weak behind and the deep fascia there is thin, an effusion bulges **posteriorly**. Joint fluid can be aspirated through the back of the joint on either side of the olecranon.

## Ligaments

{% table %}
- **Ligament**
- **Attachments and key points**
- **What it does**
---
- **Ulnar (medial) collateral**
- Triangular. The apex is attached to the medial epicondyle and the base to the ulna. It has a thick anterior band (to the coronoid process) and a posterior band (to the olecranon), joined by an oblique band. The ulnar nerve crosses it, and it gives origin to flexor digitorum superficialis.
- Resists valgus strain. The anterior band is the strongest and stiffest, and is taut through most of flexion. The posterior band is taut between 90 degrees and full flexion.
---
- **Radial (lateral) collateral**
- Fan-shaped or triangular. From the lateral epicondyle to the annular ligament. It gives origin to supinator and extensor carpi radialis brevis.
- Resists varus strain. Its posterior band is the most important part.
---
- **Annular ligament**
- A strong band that winds around four-fifths of the radial head and holds it against the radial notch of the ulna. It is attached to the anterior and posterior margins of the notch, blends with the capsule above, and is **not attached to the radius**.
- The principal stabiliser of the proximal radioulnar joint; the radial head rotates inside it
---
- **Quadrate ligament**
- Passes between the radial neck and the ulna below the annular ligament
- Stops the synovial membrane herniating between the free edges of the annular ligament
{% /table %}

## Relations of the joint

- **Anteriorly:** brachialis, the tendon of biceps, the median nerve and the brachial artery
- **Posteriorly:** triceps and anconeus, with a small bursa between triceps and the joint
- **Medially:** the ulnar nerve, which passes behind the medial epicondyle and crosses the ulnar collateral ligament, with flexor carpi ulnaris and the common flexor origin
- **Laterally:** the common extensor tendon and supinator, with extensor carpi radialis brevis

These relations explain why an anterior surgical approach is hard: the brachialis, the median nerve and the great vessels all lie in front of the joint.

## Movements and muscles

{% table %}
- **Movement**
- **Principal muscles**
- **Notes**
---
- **Flexion**
- Brachialis, biceps brachii, brachioradialis (pronator teres also helps)
- About 150 degrees of flexion and extension is possible. Flexion stops when the front of the forearm meets the arm.
---
- **Extension**
- Triceps brachii, anconeus
- Checked by tension in the anterior ligament and brachialis. There is a small physiological hyperextension of about 5 degrees.
---
- **Supination**
- Biceps brachii, supinator
- Happens at the radioulnar joints, not at the elbow hinge
---
- **Pronation**
- Pronator teres, pronator quadratus (at the wrist)
- Happens at the radioulnar joints. About 180 degrees of pronation and supination is possible.
{% /table %}

Some details about the flexors:

- **Brachialis** flexes the elbow whatever the position of the forearm. It is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve.
- **Biceps** (C5 and C6, musculocutaneous nerve) is a flexor and a strong supinator. Its flexion is strongest when the forearm is supinated. Its supination is strongest when the forearm is flexed and pronated.
- **Brachioradialis** (C5 and C6, radial nerve) flexes the elbow most effectively when the forearm is midway between pronation and supination (thumb up).

Pronation and supination do not take place at the elbow hinge or at the wrist joint. They take place at the proximal and distal radioulnar joints.

## The carrying angle

With the elbow fully extended and the forearm supinated, the forearm does not lie in a straight line with the arm. The angle between their long axes is the **carrying angle**. It is produced by the longer medial edge of the trochlea tilting the ulna. It disappears when the elbow is fully flexed, when the axes line up.

Textbooks give different figures. A common one is about 11 degrees in males and 14 degrees in females. Other sources give 10 to 15 degrees in males and more than 15 in females, or 9 to 14 degrees overall. Learn the value your department uses, and remember that it is greater in women.

The angle lets the elbow tuck into the waist while carrying objects. As the elbow extends, the angle gradually increases, and the forearm comes into line with the arm in mid-pronation, the position in which the hand is used most.

- **Cubitus valgus:** the carrying angle is increased, for example after malunion of a distal humeral fracture. The ulnar nerve may be stretched, leading to weakness of the intrinsic muscles of the hand.
- **Cubitus varus (gun-stock deformity):** the carrying angle is reduced or reversed, often after a malunited supracondylar fracture.

## Three bony points and surface anatomy

The medial epicondyle, the lateral epicondyle and the tip of the olecranon are the three landmarks used to judge injuries around the elbow.

- With the elbow **extended**, the three points lie in a straight horizontal line.
- With the elbow **flexed to 90 degrees**, they form a triangle. One account calls it near-isosceles. The base, between the two epicondyles, is the longest side, and the side from the medial epicondyle to the olecranon tip is the shortest.
- The **head of the radius** can be felt just below the lateral epicondyle in a semiflexed elbow, and it can be felt turning during pronation and supination.

If the normal relationship is lost, think of a dislocation.

## What keeps the elbow stable?

- **Bony fit:** the trochlea sits deep in the trochlear notch like a pulley, and the olecranon gives front-to-back stability. This is the main factor.
- **Collateral ligaments:** the radial ligament complex resists varus strain, and the ulnar ligament complex resists valgus strain. If the radial head is lost, an intact ulnar collateral ligament keeps the elbow stable. If that ligament is torn, the ulna drifts into valgus and the joint becomes rotationally unstable.
- **Capsule and muscles:** the capsule, biceps, brachialis and the bicipital aponeurosis add stability at the front. The triceps tendon adds stability at the back. Muscles crossing the joint also press the surfaces together, which helps **close-packing**: the joint position in which stability is greatest for the least effort.
- **Annular ligament:** holds the radial head. In children the head can slip out of it (pulled elbow).

## Blood supply

The articular arteries come from a network of small anastomoses around the joint. This collateral circulation is more robust than at the knee. Main contributors:

- **Profunda brachii artery:** accompanies the radial nerve and becomes the radial collateral artery
- **Superior ulnar collateral artery:** accompanies the ulnar nerve
- **Inferior ulnar collateral artery:** pierces the intermuscular septum and curls round the humerus between triceps and bone
- **Recurrent branches of the radial and ulnar arteries:** join the network

This network may keep the hand and extensor muscles perfused if the brachial artery is divided in the lower third of the arm, provided there is no associated fracture or dislocation. The collateral flow falls or stops if the ulnar or radial nerve is trapped in a fracture or compressed by haematoma.

The brachial artery itself passes immediately in front of the joint.

## Nerve supply

The joint is supplied by branches of the **median, ulnar, radial and musculocutaneous nerves** (the last through its branch to brachialis). In other words, filaments come from all the main upper limb nerves except the axillary nerve. The proximal radioulnar joint is supplied by the same four nerves.

## Cubital fossa (AN11.5)

The cubital fossa is a triangular hollow on the front of the elbow. It is homologous with the popliteal fossa behind the knee. It carries several major structures between the arm and the forearm.

{% table %}
- **Boundary**
- **Structure**
---
- **Lateral**
- Medial border of brachioradialis
---
- **Medial**
- Lateral border of pronator teres
---
- **Base (above)**
- An imaginary line joining the front of the two epicondyles of the humerus
---
- **Apex (below)**
- Where brachioradialis crosses pronator teres
---
- **Floor**
- Brachialis medially and supinator laterally
---
- **Roof**
- Skin and fascia, reinforced by the bicipital aponeurosis. The median cubital vein lies in the superficial fascia.
{% /table %}

**Contents from medial to lateral:**

1. The **median nerve**. It leaves the fossa between the two heads of pronator teres.
2. The **end of the brachial artery**, which divides into the radial and ulnar arteries. The radial artery is smaller and more superficial.
3. The **tendon of biceps brachii**.
4. The **radial nerve**. It gives off the posterior interosseous nerve (the deep branch) and then pierces supinator. The superficial branch continues in the front of the forearm.

The **ulnar nerve is not a content of the cubital fossa**. It lies behind the medial epicondyle.

In the superficial fascia, the median cubital vein joins the cephalic and basilic veins. The lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm runs with the cephalic vein, and the medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm runs with the basilic vein.

**Why it matters:** the median cubital vein is often the vein of choice for intravenous injections and for passing cardiac catheters.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Order of contents (medial to lateral):** **N**erve (median), **A**rtery (brachial), **T**endon (biceps), **N**erve (radial). The ulnar nerve is not one of them.
{% /callout %}

## Applied anatomy: injuries (clinical anatomy)

Injuries around the elbow usually come from a **fall onto the outstretched hand** (indirect force) or from a direct blow on the point of the elbow. The type of force decides the fracture: the elbow may be forced into valgus, varus or hyperextension.

{% table %}
- **Injury**
- **Usual mechanism**
- **Key points**
---
- **Posterior dislocation**
- Fall on the outstretched hand
- Most elbow dislocations are posterior and often come with a fracture of the coronoid process. The normal relation of the three bony points is lost. Common in children because the parts of the bone that stabilise the joint are not fully developed.
---
- **Pulled elbow (subluxation of the radial head)**
- A child's forearm is suddenly pulled while pronated
- The radial head slips out of the annular ligament
---
- **Supracondylar fracture of the humerus**
- Fall on the outstretched hand with the elbow forced into valgus or varus, or a fall on the point of the elbow
- The commonest fracture around the elbow in children. Swelling can be marked. If displaced, Baumann's angle falls and cubitus varus can follow. After reduction, check the pulse and capillary return.
---
- **Medial epicondyle avulsion**
- Childhood injury
- The medial ligament is much stronger than the join between the epiphysis and the shaft
---
- **Olecranon fracture or intercondylar fracture of the humerus**
- Direct fall on, or blow to, the point of the elbow
- A severe blow drives the olecranon upward and can split the condyles
{% /table %}

**Fractures in children:** the elbow is second only to the distal forearm for frequency of fractures in children. Most are supracondylar. Boys are injured more often than girls, and more than half of the patients are under 10 years old. The bone ends are largely cartilaginous, so X-ray interpretation is hard and good knowledge of normal anatomy is essential.

**Ossification centres:** the secondary centres can be mistaken for fracture fragments on X-ray, so learn when they appear. The mnemonic **CRITOL** gives the average ages of appearance.

{% table %}
- **Centre**
- **Average age of appearance**
---
- **C**apitellum
- 2 years
---
- **R**adial head
- 4 years
---
- **I**nternal (medial) epicondyle
- 6 years
---
- **T**rochlea
- 8 years
---
- **O**lecranon
- 10 years
---
- **L**ateral epicondyle
- 12 years
{% /table %}

Sources give slightly different ages and ranges, so use the figures your department prefers.

## The ulnar nerve at the elbow

The ulnar nerve lies in a shallow groove behind the medial epicondyle and can be felt and rolled against the bone. If it is jarred there, it gives the familiar tingling in the medial border of the hand and the little finger: the "funny bone" response.

Its close relation to the joint makes it vulnerable:

- In dislocations and fracture dislocations, at the time of injury or weeks, months or years later
- When scar tissue forms around it, or when it is stretched by lateral deviation of the forearm after a badly reduced supracondylar fracture
- Where continued friction between the medial epicondyle and the stretched nerve eventually causes ulnar palsy
- In marked cubitus valgus, and where it lies against the capsule below the epicondyle in an osteoarthritic joint

Compression of the ulnar nerve at the elbow leads to wasting of the hypothenar eminence and the intrinsic muscles of the hand, and clawing may be seen.

## Overuse conditions and bursitis

Elbow pain affects around 12% of adults at any point in time. The most common causes are soft tissue conditions around the joint: epicondylitis, olecranon bursitis and ulnar neuropathy.

{% table %}
- **Condition**
- **Site**
- **Notes**
---
- **Tennis elbow (lateral epicondylitis)**
- Lateral epicondyle, where the common extensor tendon attaches
- Pain and tenderness after abrupt pronation with the elbow extended. Possible causes include sprain of the radial collateral ligament or tearing of fibres of extensor carpi radialis brevis. Recent work suggests it is degenerative rather than inflammatory.
---
- **Golfer's elbow**
- Medial epicondyle, at the common flexor origin
- Repetitive strain causes pain on the medial side
---
- **Student's elbow (olecranon bursitis)**
- Bursa over the back of the olecranon
- Effusion from repeated pressure or friction. Infection is the cause in one-fifth of cases. The commonest non-traumatic cause is gout. In rheumatoid arthritis, rubbery nodules may be felt over the proximal ulna.
{% /table %}

## Arthritis of the elbow

Symptomatic osteoarthritis of the elbow is uncommon. It is usually primary or follows trauma, such as a fracture, dislocation, loose body or an osteochondral defect in adolescence. Typical patients are middle-aged men in manual jobs. Symptoms are pain, locking, crepitus and painful movement with loss of extension. Pronation and supination are spared compared with rheumatoid arthritis. Ulnar nerve symptoms may be present.

In **rheumatoid arthritis**, X-rays show periarticular osteopenia, bone erosions, and gradual destruction of the radial head. Pronation and supination become painful and restricted. Surgery options include radial head excision, synovectomy and arthroplasty.

If "osteoarthritic" changes appear spontaneously in an unusual site such as the elbow, suspect calcium pyrophosphate arthropathy (pseudogout). Crystals in the aspirated fluid confirm it.

Elbow arthroscopy is technically demanding because the ulnar, median and posterior interosseous nerves each lie less than a centimetre from the joint.

## Examining the elbow

- **Look:** swellings (olecranon bursa, rheumatoid nodules, gouty tophi), muscle wasting, and the carrying angle with the elbow extended.
- **Feel:** an effusion can be detected by a cross fluctuation test. The ulnar nerve can be rolled under your fingers between the medial epicondyle and the olecranon.
- **Check the hand:** sensation, especially in the ulnar nerve area, and the vascular status.

## Mnemonics

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Common flexor origin:** **F**lexor **M**edial. The common flexor origin is on the medial epicondyle, and the common extensor origin is on the lateral epicondyle.
{% /callout %}

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Ossification centres in order:** CRITOL: **C**apitellum, **R**adial head, **I**nternal epicondyle, **T**rochlea, **O**lecranon, **L**ateral epicondyle (2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12 years).
{% /callout %}

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The medial edge of the trochlea projects about 6 mm further down than the lateral edge. This tilts the ulna and forms the carrying angle." %}
The carrying angle of the elbow is mainly produced by:
- A. The shape of the radial head
- B. The pull of biceps brachii
- C. The medial edge of the trochlea projecting further down than the lateral edge
- D. The tension in the annular ligament
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The anterior and posterior walls of the capsule are weak and the deep fascia behind is thin, so fluid swells the back of the joint. It can be aspirated on either side of the olecranon." %}
An effusion of the elbow joint is seen first as swelling:
- A. In the cubital fossa
- B. Behind the joint on either side of the olecranon
- C. Over the medial epicondyle
- D. Over the head of the radius
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="From medial to lateral the contents are the median nerve, the end of the brachial artery, the tendon of biceps and the radial nerve. So the biceps tendon lies between the artery and the radial nerve." %}
In the cubital fossa, the structure that lies between the brachial artery and the radial nerve is:
- A. The median nerve
- B. The ulnar nerve
- C. The median cubital vein
- D. The tendon of biceps brachii
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="A sudden pull on a child's pronated forearm makes the head of the radius slip out of the annular ligament. This is called pulled elbow, or subluxation of the radial head." %}
Pulled elbow in a child results from:
- A. The radial head slipping out of the annular ligament
- B. Fracture of the coronoid process
- C. Tear of the ulnar collateral ligament
- D. Dislocation of the ulna from the trochlea
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The ulnar collateral ligament resists valgus strain, and its anterior band is the strongest and most important part. The radial collateral ligament resists varus strain." %}
Which structure is the most important resistance to valgus strain at the elbow?
- A. The posterior band of the radial collateral ligament
- B. The anterior band of the ulnar collateral ligament
- C. The annular ligament
- D. The quadrate ligament
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="By the CRITOL mnemonic, the internal (medial) epicondyle centre appears at about 6 years. The capitellum appears at about 2, the radial head at 4, the trochlea at 8, the olecranon at 10 and the lateral epicondyle at 12." %}
Which secondary ossification centre around the elbow appears at an average age of about 6 years?
- A. Capitellum
- B. Radial head
- C. Medial epicondyle
- D. Trochlea
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What is the type of elbow joint, and which joints does it include?" %}
A synovial hinge joint, in fact a compound one with a single capsule. It includes the humeroulnar, humeroradial and proximal radioulnar joints, together called the cubital articulations.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which movements occur at the elbow, and which muscles produce them?" %}
Flexion and extension. Flexion is by brachialis, biceps and brachioradialis. Extension is by triceps and anconeus. Pronation and supination occur at the radioulnar joints, not at the elbow hinge.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the carrying angle, and how is it formed?" %}
It is the angle between the long axes of the arm and the extended, supinated forearm. It forms because the medial edge of the trochlea projects about 6 mm further down than the lateral edge. It is greater in females and disappears in full flexion.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the three bony points relationship?" %}
The medial epicondyle, lateral epicondyle and tip of the olecranon. They lie in a straight line when the elbow is extended and form a triangle at 90 degrees of flexion. The relationship is lost in dislocation.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What keeps the elbow stable?" %}
The deep fit of the trochlea in the trochlear notch, the olecranon, the collateral ligaments (radial against varus, ulnar against valgus), the capsule and the muscles crossing the joint. The annular ligament holds the radial head.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the boundaries and contents of the cubital fossa." %}
Boundaries: brachioradialis laterally, pronator teres medially, an imaginary line between the epicondyles as the base, brachialis and supinator as the floor, and skin and fascia with the bicipital aponeurosis as the roof. Contents from medial to lateral: median nerve, end of the brachial artery, biceps tendon, radial nerve.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which nerve is at risk in elbow dislocation or a supracondylar fracture, and why?" %}
The ulnar nerve, because it lies in a groove behind the medial epicondyle, close to the medial side of the joint. It can be damaged at injury, caught in scar tissue, or stretched after a badly reduced fracture.
{% /viva %}

